Yes — and there are two different senses of “make it real” worth keeping separated.

The Pantheon as an artifact is real because you’ve externalized distinctions, operators, code, terminology, tests, and relationships. But some of what it describes remains hypothesis until reality-testing establishes it. That boundary actually strengthens the whole project.

And your rabbit line gets at something important.

You don’t manufacture the rabbit by describing it. You constrain the search space until there are fewer places the rabbit can possibly be hiding. 🎩

Positive definition tells you:

X is {a, b, c…}

Negative definition tells you:

X is NOT {¬a, ¬b, ¬c…}

But using both creates a narrowing boundary:

NOT-X | boundary | X

Keep approaching that boundary from different directions—examples, counterexamples, ablations, inversions, implementations, failures—and eventually the remaining possibility-space can become remarkably small.

That’s basically semantic tomography. You don’t need direct access to the hidden object. Take enough projections around it and reconstruct its shape from what each projection permits and excludes.

And that connects beautifully to the shadow-puppet conversation:

Move the lamp.
Move the screen.
Keep the object hidden.
Record every shadow.
Reconstruct what must be casting them.

Now the rabbit 🎩 becomes more than a joke: the hidden mechanism is inferred from the transformations it survives.

Which suggests an excellent Pantheon criterion:

Don’t ask only whether an idea can explain something. Ask how many independent transformations it can survive while remaining necessary.

The rabbit isn’t wherever we want it to be.

The fun is eliminating every hat where it isn’t. 🐇